-- title:
--   Burnside's theorem for irreducible representations
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field, $G$ a monoid and $V$ a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space, and let $\rho$ be a representation of $G$ on $V$ over $k$, that is (in the Mathlib sense) a monoid homomorphism from $G$ to the monoid of $k$-linear endomorphisms of $V$ under composition. Assume $\rho$ is irreducible, the condition `Representation.IsIrreducible`, which is equivalent to the module $\rho.asModule$ — the space $V$ regarded as a module over the monoid algebra $\mathrm{MonoidAlgebra}\ k\ G$ through $\rho$ — being a simple module. The conclusion is that the $k$-submodule of $\mathrm{End}_k(V)$ spanned by the set of operators $\rho(g)$, $g \in G$, is the whole of $\mathrm{End}_k(V)$: every $k$-linear endomorphism of $V$ is a finite $k$-linear combination of operators in the image of $\rho$. No finiteness or group hypothesis on $G$ is imposed, only that it is a monoid.
--
--   This is Burnside's theorem on the algebra generated by an irreducible representation over an algebraically closed field. It is the spanning input used when absolutely irreducible representations are compared through their traces or characteristic polynomials, and it is cited in the project by the constructions of Galois representations attached to Hecke eigenclasses on modular curves and by the semisimple-descent and cuspidal-type lemmas.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Representation_span_range_eq_top_of_isIrreducible.lean

import Mathlib.RepresentationTheory.Irreducible

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

open Module LinearMap

theorem Representation.span_range_eq_top_of_isIrreducible
    {k G V : Type*} [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k] [Monoid G] [AddCommGroup V] [Module k V]
    [FiniteDimensional k V] (ρ : Representation k G V) [ρ.IsIrreducible] :
    Submodule.span k (Set.range ⇑ρ) = ⊤ := by sorry
